If you want the robot to change direction when the bumper is pressed, you can modify the existing code to include additional movements, such as turning left or right. This approach uses the S-R-A (Sensing-Reasoning-Acting) loop principles, ensuring that your robot responds dynamically to obstacles.

### Reference to Course Material:
This task relates to the "Sensing-Reasoning-Acting Loop" and actuator control for direction changes【8:4†source】.

### Explanation Using the Course Context:
Assuming you already have the basic bumper sensor code, you can enhance it as follows. The following code will make the robot move forward until the bumper is pressed, at which point it will turn in a specified direction (left or right).

```cpp
#include <RobotMovingFunctions.h>
const int BUMPER_PIN = A0;

void setup() {
    setIOpins();
    pinMode(BUMPER_PIN, INPUT);
}

void loop() {
    bool bumperIsPressed = digitalRead(BUMPER_PIN); // Read bumper state

    if (bumperIsPressed) {
        // Stop when the bumper is pressed
        stopTheRobot();
        delay(500);  // Brief pause

        // Change direction: turn right
        robotRight(); // Adjust this to robotLeft() if you want it to turn left
        delay(1000);  // Turn for 1 second

        moveForward(); // Continue moving forward after the turn
    } 
    else {
        moveForward(); // Keep moving forward if bumper not pressed
    }
}
```

### Connection to a Concrete Task or Experiment:
After implementing the above code, test it with your robot. Observe how the robot responds when the bumper is pressed and whether it successfully turns in the chosen direction. You might also want to experiment by changing the turn direction or the duration of the turn to see how it affects the robot's behavior.

### Knowledge Check:
- What behavior do you expect when the bumper is pressed?
- If you wanted the robot to back up instead of turning, how would you modify the code?
- Why is it important to include a delay after the bumper is pressed before changing direction?

These questions will help you reflect on your understanding of the logic behind the programmed behavior and how it relates to the S-R-A loop in robotic programming.
